CAVEAT:  The DNS forwarding cache limb (S1570.1.03) is UNMEASURED. With the cache enabled
         (`DNS Cache: enabled  Size: 100  Max-TTL: 60 second(s)`), the SAME name queried
         twice 5.3 s apart -- well inside the 60 s TTL, upstream returning ttl 60 both times
         -- produced TWO upstream queries, where a cache hit should have made the second
         absent:
             17:10:24.518819 IP 192.168.48.100.30837 > 192.168.48.9.53: A? ck-cache.test.
             17:10:29.818847 IP 192.168.48.100.40305 > 192.168.48.9.53: A? ck-cache.test.
         This is NOT graded as a failure. The TFS describes two distinct processes, a Name
         Resolver Process for local applications and a DNS Relay Process for forwarding, and
         S1570.1.03 reads "Caches DNS name resolve result" while S1570.1.11 reads "Configures
         name resolve result cache size" -- wording that plausibly scopes the cache to the
         name-resolver path, the device's own lookups, rather than to relayed third-party
         queries. If that reading is right, forwarding every relayed query upstream is correct
         behaviour and the test premise was wrong. No text was found that settles it either
         way, and nothing on this bench can distinguish "the relay path is not cached by
         design" from "the cache is not working". The limb needs a developer's answer rather
         than another bench run. It does not affect the verdict, which rests on the forwarding
         limbs measured cleanly at both ends.
CAVEAT:  The name-server preference measured in STEP 4 is an EXTERNAL observation. It
         establishes which server this DUT actually queries; it cannot establish the internal
         order of the name-server list that S1570.1.12's "added last" describes, and
         `show ip name-server` cannot either because it groups by type.

